  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1232 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nOn Friday, global models depict multiple areas of low pressure \nacross the Gulf and western Atlantic. Our original area of low \npressure remains in the Gulf while an additional lobe of \nvorticity is able to break off into the western Atlantic and \nbecomes the dominant feature. Across the eastern CONUS, high \npressure at the surface begins to breakdown as a cold front sweeps\nacross the central United States. Before the arrival of drier air for\nthe weekend, perhaps one additional day of heavy rainfall\npotential on Friday, especially earlier in the day as PWATs remain\nmaximized and will still approach 2\". Exact forecast for Friday\nwill be heavily dependent on the evolution of the surface lows,\nhowever, so I would place confidence on this portion of the\nforecast on the lower end for now. The cold front sweeping across\nthe central CONUS and retreat of high pressure will help pull the\nlow over the western Atlantic towards the north and finally usher\nin drier air across the area through the weekend timeframe. As \nthis system deepens and races off to the north, PWATs drop into \nthe 1.2-1.3\" range and Saturday and continue to fall to perhaps \nbelow 1\" on Sunday and Monday. This should help limit rain chances\nthrough the weekend, although there will still be a chance \n(10-20%) for scattered shower activity each day through the \nweekend and early next week. \n\nMoisture begins to return heading towards the middle portion of the \nweek as an area of low pressure slides across the Gulf states. This \nwill likely be our next shot for more significant rainfall although \ndetails will become clearer over the coming days.",
